# SecurityInsight -- public repo .gitignore
#
# Protects customer secrets + run output from being:
#   - accidentally committed via `git add .`
#   - destroyed by `git clean -fd`
#
# This file ships to the public repo via the AutomateIT publish workflow
# (the source AutomateIT/.gitignore covers the same paths from the monorepo
# side; this file covers them from the customer-VM side).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Customer secrets / config -- NEVER commit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config/SecurityInsight.custom.ps1
launcher/*/LauncherConfig.custom.ps1
# Your own risk index rows (read before the shipped riskscore.index.locked.csv). Until 3.0 this name was the shipped
# index; it is never shipped now, so a git pull can never overwrite or stop on it.
risk-analysis-detection/riskscore.index.custom.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Developer-only experimental queries -- NEVER sync to customers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RiskAnalysis_Queries_Dev.yaml lets a developer stage problematic queries
# locally (engine merges it BETWEEN locked and custom). Customers must never
# pull this file via the public-mirror publish workflow.
risk-analysis-detection/RiskAnalysis_Queries_Dev.yaml
risk-analysis-detection/dev/RiskAnalysis_Queries_Dev.yaml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Run output -- regenerated each run, no value in git
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
logs/
OUTPUT/
# The build stamp (engine/_shared/SI-BuildInfo.ps1) is written at image/package build time, never committed: a committed
# one would say the wrong build for every later commit.
/BUILD.json
DATA/
staging/
SOLUTIONS/SecurityInsight/DATA/
SOLUTIONS/SecurityInsight/LOGS/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Tracked: privilege-tier-catalog.locked.json
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The catalog JSON is a generic Microsoft-roles -> tier mapping (built by
# PrivilegeTierClassifier from Azure OpenAI, then reviewed). It ships in the
# repo so customers get tier-definition updates via `git pull` and the
# Identity engine works on first run with no extra steps. Customers who
# want to customise tier assignments for their tenant can re-run
# PrivilegeTierClassifier locally; the regenerated file will show as
# modified in `git status` (expected).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Local IDE / OS noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vs/
.idea/
*.suo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Safety-net backups produced by the 2026-06-11 .custom.yaml description-block
# repair pass. Keep them for diff but never commit.
*.corrupted

# Per-tenant asset-tagging knobs (AutoExcludeRules.*). Never commit -- belongs only
# in the operator's working copy. Sample stays tracked for schema reference.
config/asset-tagging.config.json


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CUSTOMER AUTHORITY OVERLAYS -- the "custom" half of every locked+custom pair in config/.
#
# Listed individually until 2026-08-31, which meant each NEW overlay had to remember to add
# itself: cmdb-source-ownership.custom.json, correlation-decisions.custom.yaml and
# asset-decisions.custom.yaml were all committable by omission. A pattern cannot forget.
#
# 🔑 `*.custom.json` does NOT match `*.custom.sample.json`, so the shipped samples -- which are the
# schema reference customers copy from -- stay tracked. That distinction is the whole reason this is
# three narrow patterns rather than one broad `*custom*`.
config/*.custom.json
config/*.custom.yaml
config/*.custom.ps1

# v3 data model: customer overrides of the data-model templates live on the customer, never in the repo
data-model/**/*.custom.json
